; On the virtual clock, an answer on its way isn't beaten by a timer
; (D236). A command, its input left open so that time doesn't stand
; still for it (D185), is sent a line, and echoes it in its own time,
; a moment later, for real; meanwhile a timer is pending, due in a
; second. The answer comes first, and the clock hasn't moved. Before,
; the clock went straight to the timer whenever the answer wasn't there
; the instant it looked, as on macOS's loopback, or from another
; program, every time.

(defun asker ()
    (recv
        ((:open p)   (send p (list :write "ping" \n)) (after 1000 $$ :timeout) (asker))
        ((:line p s) (list s (clock/us)))
        (:timeout    (list :timeout (clock/us)))))

(pprint (join (connect :exec (list "sh" "-c" "read line; echo got $line; read rest") (asker))))
